title:: CamaraObscura
summary:: Advanced algorithmic reverberation models

image::camara_obscura_banner.png::

summary:: Prepared-model reverberation architectures for SuperCollider
categories:: Libraries>CamaraObscura
related:: Classes/DarkVelvetReverb, Classes/GeometryReverb, Classes/GroupedFDN, Classes/ModalPlate, Classes/ModalReverbBank, Classes/RIRFDN, Classes/VelvetFDN

description::

CamaraObscura contains seven wet-only feedforward,
feedback-delay-network, modal, physical plate, measured-late-field, and
prepared geometric reverberators. It is an experimental adaptation
project: techniques come from the cited acoustic literature, while the
prepared-model ABI, safety boundaries, compact implementations, controls, and
tools are independent GPLv3 work.

Every renderer has a corresponding model, profile, or scene class. Preparation
validates values and creates a versioned mono Buffer containing family magic,
version, total size, sample rate, channel count, flags, seed, four family
fields, and payload. The server validates that format again, retains fixed
state for the Synth lifetime, and becomes silent if its Buffer identity or
storage changes. Treat a loaded model Buffer as immutable: free or replace the
Synth before modifying or reusing that Buffer.

All UGens return wet audio only. Add the dry signal outside the UGen so
latency and routing remain explicit. Recursive FDN and generic modal classes
provide stable freeze; every renderer provides allocation-free reset according
to its public API. No calculation callback allocates, performs file I/O,
factorizes a matrix, constructs an FFT plan, parses a model, waits, locks, or
logs.

The collection is experimental. Read each class page for exact construction
parameters, sample-rate matching, stability, latency, memory, reset/freeze,
and output channel behavior.

section:: Classes

link::Classes/DarkVelvetReverb::
Multichannel feedforward late reverb directly convolving audio with sparse, filtered dark velvet noise pulses.

link::Classes/GeometryReverb::
Interpolates direct paths and six first-order shoebox reflections with a shared late-field FDN core.

link::Classes/GroupedFDN::
Models interconnected spaces using coupled feedback delay networks, keeping energy transfers unconditionally stable via Givens rotations.

link::Classes/ModalPlate::
Physically models the vibration modes of a rectangular plate, allowing real-time traversal of excitation and pickup coordinates.

link::Classes/ModalReverbBank::
Constructs a reverberant tail entirely out of thousands of independent ringing resonators, allowing extreme pitch and dispersion modulation.

link::Classes/RIRFDN::
Fits a parametric Feedback Delay Network to an offline Schroeder analysis of a real room impulse response.

link::Classes/VelvetFDN::
Uses paraunitary velvet noise sequences within the feedback loops to create an instantly dense, wash-like tail.

section:: Visual Comparison of Impulse Responses

Plotting the Impulse Responses (IRs) of the different algorithmic structures is one of the clearest ways to understand how they differ. Below are the offline rendered impulse responses (0.5 seconds each) for the 7 reverbs, visually demonstrating their unique temporal envelopes, echo densities, and dispersion characteristics.

image::ir_darkvelvet.png#DarkVelvetReverb shows randomized, sparse discrete pulses with a slowly decaying exponential envelope.::

image::ir_geometry.png#GeometryReverb shows distinct high-amplitude early reflections preceding the diffuse late tail.::

image::ir_grouped.png#GroupedFDN shows the energy sloshing between sub-networks, creating a complex non-linear decay envelope.::

image::ir_plate.png#ModalPlate shows the high-frequency sinusoidal beating characteristic of a ringing physical structure.::

image::ir_modalbank.png#ModalReverbBank shows a sparse mix of independent beating resonances.::

image::ir_rirfdn.png#RIRFDN shows an instantly smooth and dense exponential decay modeled from a real space.::

image::ir_velvetfdn.png#VelvetFDN shows an immediate, thick block of solid density that bypasses the slow build-up of traditional FDNs.::

section:: Workflow

Create the model only after the server sample rate is known, load it, and wait for the Buffer before starting the Synth. Evaluating this step-by-step is the safest approach:

code::
// 1. Boot server
s.boot;

// 2. Prepare the model
~model = GroupedFDNModel.twoRooms(0.5, 4, 0.2, s.sampleRate, 2).load(s);

// 3. Play the synth (run this after the model buffer has loaded)
(
x = {
    var fade = EnvGen.kr(Env([0, 1], [0.08]));
    GroupedFDN.ar(Impulse.ar(0.5), ~model, 2, 4)[0] * fade * 0.4
}.play;
)

// 4. Cleanup
x.free; ~model.free;
::

Prepared files are portable text and can be inspected with
code::tools/model_inspect/model_inspect.py::. The deterministic
code::tools/rir2fdn/rir2fdn.py:: analyzer creates late-only RIR models,
verification WAV, and a fit report. code::ModalReverbModel.fromIR:: invokes
the installed dependency-free modal extractor synchronously outside the audio
server.

section:: Deliberate limits

Dark Velvet is decorrelated rather than binaural and has 4096 samples of
latency. Group coupling is full-band while group losses are two-band.
RIRFDN reproduces only broad late decay and coloration. Modal extraction is
FFT-peak based rather than high-resolution system identification. ModalPlate
supports only simply supported rectangles. GeometryReverb supports shoebox
direct/first-order paths and a stereo late field, not arbitrary meshes,
diffraction, occlusion, elevation, or HRTFs.

HOADirectionalFDN is outside this project. ScatteringDelayReverb is not built
or documented as available because its legal gate remains unresolved.

section:: Verification and performance

The project includes strict C++ reference tests, deterministic offline-tool
tests, model round trips, installed-server family and combined stress tests,
sanitizer builds, supernova builds, documentation rendering, and native
benchmarks. Benchmark results always state machine, compiler, precision,
nominal sample rate, measured kernel, and exclusions; they are not presented
as a universal quality score.

section:: Plotting Impulse Responses in SuperCollider

If you wish to visualize the impulse response of any reverb yourself natively within the SuperCollider IDE, you can easily shoot a single impulse through the UGen, record the output to a temporary buffer, and use the `.plot` method.

code::
// 1. Boot server
s.boot;

// 2. Prepare models and allocate a 3-channel buffer for 1 second of audio
(
~rirModel = RIRFDNModel.synthetic(sampleRate: s.sampleRate).load(s);
~vfdnModel = VelvetFDNModel.default(8, 3, s.sampleRate).load(s);
~grpModel = GroupedFDNModel.twoRooms(0.5, 4, 0.2, s.sampleRate, 2).load(s);
~buf = Buffer.alloc(s, s.sampleRate.asInteger, 3);
)

// 3. Record the impulse responses
(
~recSynth = {
    var imp = Impulse.ar(0);
    var sig = [
        RIRFDN.ar(imp, ~rirModel)[0],
        VelvetFDN.ar(imp, ~vfdnModel)[0],
        GroupedFDN.ar(imp, ~grpModel)[0]
    ];
    RecordBuf.ar(sig, ~buf, loop: 0, doneAction: 2);
    Line.kr(0, 1, 1, doneAction: 2); // Free the synth after 1 second
}.play;
)

// 4. Plot them together (run this after recording finishes)
(
~buf.loadToFloatArray(action: { |array|
    defer {
        // Separate the interleaved buffer array into 3 distinct channels
        var channels = array.clump(3).flop;
        var plt = channels.plot("Impulse Responses: RIRFDN (top), VelvetFDN (mid), GroupedFDN (bot)");
        plt.superpose_(false); // Stack them rather than overlaying
    };
});
)

// 5. Cleanup
~buf.free; ~rirModel.free; ~vfdnModel.free; ~grpModel.free;
::

